<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georgios Papandreou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1888">1888</a>–<a href="/years/1968">1968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as George Papandreou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Papandreou was a Greek liberal politician who led the government in exile and returned to liberated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 1944. He founded the Centre Union party, which won the elections of 1963 and 1964, and as Prime Minister he clashed with the young King Constantine II over control of the army, leading to his dismissal in July 1965. He was under house arrest during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a> and died in 1968.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georgios-papandreou.jpg" alt="Georgios Papandreou" width="320" height="519" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georgios_Papandreu_(Papandreou).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Papandreou was born Georgios Stavropoulos at Kalentzi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, the son of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> archpriest, Father Andreas Stavropoulos. He studied law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and political science in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, and his thinking was strongly influenced by German social democracy. He opposed the monarchy and the communists. He volunteered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a>, and the Liberal leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleftherios-venizelos">Eleftherios Venizelos</a> made him governor of Chios. In 1916 he accompanied Venizelos to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and then rallied support on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> for Venizelos's insurgent government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a> against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He was imprisoned in 1921 for an article calling on the king to abdicate, and again under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoros-pangalos">Theodoros Pangalos</a>. As education minister in 1930 to 1932 he reformed the school system and built many schools for refugees' children. In 1938 the dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannis-metaxas">Ioannis Metaxas</a> exiled him. After Italian forces imprisoned him in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and in 1944 headed the government in exile. Under his premiership came the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> conference of May 1944 and the Caserta Agreement of September 1944. He entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in October as prime minister of a government of national unity and resigned in January 1945 after the Dekemvriana, the conflict between EAM and non-EAM forces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1961 Papandreou founded the Centre Union, which won a minority vote in 1963 and formed a government after the elections of 1964. His government made free education available at all levels and lengthened compulsory schooling. His relationship with the young King Constantine II worsened, and when the defence minister Petros Garoufalias refused to leave his post over the ASPIDA scandal in the army, Papandreou clashed with the king and resigned in July 1965. The coup of April 1967 installed a military regime, and Papandreou was put under house arrest. He died on 1 November 1968. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andreas-papandreou">Andreas Papandreou</a> later became prime minister.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Georgios Papandreou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1946 the restored monarchy ruled through conservative governments under Alexandros Papagos and Konstantinos Karamanlis, with left-wing parties restricted, until the crisis of 1965 between King Constantine II and <b>George Papandreou</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In Greece the clash between Constantine II of Greece and the prime minister <b>George Papandreou</b> in 1965 opened a period of instability that ended in the colonels’ coup of 1967, and in 1974 Greeks voted to abolish the monarchy.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Lebanon Conference in May 1944, PEEA agreed to join a government of national unity under <b>Georgios Papandreou</b>, and its ministers entered that government in September.</blockquote><a href="/state/political-committee-of-national-liberation">Political Committee of National Liberation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kalentzi, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.95,21.76&amp;year=1940" aria-label="Kalentzi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Georgios Papandreou"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4446 4302h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4446 4302h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.95,21.76&amp;year=1940">What was here in 1940? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
